 of consoles coupled with  high expectations for performer  and polish have resulted in many  developers preferring to play it safe  and extend their development time even  when their publishers have already hyped  to release dates which will probably to  put it plainly unrealistic stitching  release dates could go a couple of  different ways one of which was made  famous by Starcraft and Warcraft  developer Blizzard where developers tell  game is that a game will be out when  it's ready if there's a game being made  from the developer whose games you know  you like sometimes it's enough just to  know that something is coming it's a  strategy being used by a few companies  at the moment while developers setup  react Brad has had some trouble with The  Witcher 3 now on its second delay news  on their other upcoming RPGs cyberpunk  2077 has been very quiet and that's not  necessarily a bad thing  despite being announced before the  witcher 3 cyberpunk 2077 has been in  development out of the public eye over  the last few years aside from some  concept art and the announcements  trailer which closed with coming up when  it's ready we've also seen EA do a  little bit of this concerning Mass  Effect 4 they've told us it's still  coming and showing us some concept arts  but they haven't such a big coming 2015  on it or anything to get our hopes up  eventually these games will have their  release date announced and a big  marketing push will start up but the  idea that these games are happy to be  chugging along under the radar for a  while until they're good to go is better  than saying this year wait no next year  ok the year after  instead gamers are contented to think we  know it's coming just make it good it  might not be what publishers want but  honesty and an unwillingness to wedge a  game into a deadline so early in the dev  process is way more respectable the  third option is a slightly controversial  one it's where Deb's open up the  development process to peopl